insert into public.flashcards (id, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('f3-056','core',3,'3.1',$$What is container orchestration and what problem does it solve?$$,$$Automates deploying, scaling, networking, and healing many containers across a cluster (e.g., Kubernetes) - manual container management doesn't scale past a handful of instances.$$),
('f3-057','core',3,'3.1',$$What is multi-tenancy risk in public cloud?$$,$$Multiple customers share the same underlying physical hardware; a hypervisor flaw or misconfiguration could theoretically let one tenant's workload affect another's - mitigated by the provider's isolation controls.$$),
('f3-058','core',3,'3.1',$$What is edge computing and its security trade-off?$$,$$Processing data closer to its source (IoT gateways, regional nodes) reduces latency but multiplies the number of physically distributed, often less-secured locations needing protection.$$),
('f3-059','core',3,'3.1',$$What is a serverless "cold start" and any security relevance?$$,$$The delay when a function spins up from zero instances; ephemeral execution environments mean less persistent attack surface but also complicate forensics after an incident.$$),
('f3-060','core',3,'3.1',$$Physical vs virtual vs cloud infrastructure - how does security ownership shift?$$,$$Physical: you own and secure everything. Virtual (on-prem hypervisor): you still secure the hypervisor and above. Cloud: shared responsibility shifts hardware/facility security to the provider.$$),
('f3-061','core',3,'3.2',$$What is Software-Defined Networking (SDN)?$$,$$Separates the network's control plane (deciding how traffic flows) from the data plane (forwarding it), enabling centralized, programmable network policy instead of per-device configuration.$$),
('f3-062','core',3,'3.2',$$Why does east-west traffic control matter for Zero Trust?$$,$$North-south crosses the perimeter (traditionally well-guarded); east-west is lateral movement between internal hosts - Zero Trust insists on inspecting and authenticating east-west traffic too, not just the border.$$),
('f3-063','core',3,'3.2',$$What does an API gateway provide for security?$$,$$A single controlled entry point for API traffic: authentication, rate limiting, request validation, and logging - centralizing controls that would otherwise be duplicated per microservice.$$),
('f3-064','core',3,'3.2',$$ZTNA vs traditional VPN?$$,$$VPN grants broad network-level access once connected. ZTNA grants per-application, per-session access after continuous identity/context verification - smaller blast radius if compromised.$$),
('f3-065','core',3,'3.2',$$What is microsegmentation?$$,$$Fine-grained network policy applied per-workload (not just per-subnet), often software-defined, so even two servers in the same segment can't talk unless explicitly allowed.$$),
('f3-066','core',3,'3.3',$$What are the three common places DLP inspects?$$,$$Endpoint (USB, clipboard, print), network (email, web uploads), and cloud (SaaS/CASB integration) - covering data wherever it can leave.$$),
('f3-067','core',3,'3.3',$$What is the "right to be forgotten" and its architecture challenge?$$,$$A data subject's right to have their personal data deleted; challenging in systems with backups, replicas, and analytics pipelines where the data was copied many times over.$$),
('f3-068','core',3,'3.3',$$What is envelope encryption's role in key management at scale?$$,$$A data key encrypts the actual data; a master key (rarely used, tightly controlled) encrypts only the small data key - limiting exposure of the most sensitive key while scaling encryption operations.$$),
('f3-069','core',3,'3.3',$$What is a key management lifecycle?$$,$$Generation (secure random creation), usage, scheduled rotation (limits exposure window), and retirement/destruction when no longer needed or compromised - keys aren't "set and forget."$$),
('f3-070','core',3,'3.4',$$COOP vs DRP - overlap and difference?$$,$$Continuity of Operations Planning keeps essential business FUNCTIONS running broadly during any disruption; DRP is the narrower IT-systems-restoration plan, often a supporting piece of COOP/BCP.$$),
('f3-071','core',3,'3.4',$$Parallel processing test vs full interruption test?$$,$$Parallel: recovery systems run alongside production to validate without risk. Full interruption: production is actually stopped and failover is used for real - highest confidence, highest risk.$$),
('f3-072','core',3,'3.4',$$Capacity planning: what is the people dimension?$$,$$Ensuring enough trained staff AND cross-training exist so one person's absence or departure doesn't eliminate the only person who can execute recovery procedures.$$),
('f3-073','core',3,'3.4',$$What is a recovery time objective vs a maximum tolerable downtime?$$,$$RTO is the target you engineer toward; MTD is the hard ceiling the business can survive. RTO must be set safely below MTD, with margin for things not going perfectly.$$),
('f3-074','core',3,'3.1',$$What is Infrastructure as Code drift and how is it prevented?$$,$$When live infrastructure is manually changed outside the IaC pipeline, it no longer matches the source-controlled definition; prevented by disallowing manual changes and re-applying IaC to correct drift.$$),
('f3-075','core',3,'3.2',$$What is a reverse proxy's security benefit beyond load balancing?$$,$$It hides backend server details (IPs, versions) from the internet, centralizes TLS termination and WAF inspection, and can rate-limit or block malicious requests before they reach app servers.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('q3-046',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A company runs 200 containers across a fleet of servers and needs automatic scaling, self-healing, and service discovery without manual intervention. What category of tool do they need?$q$,$q$["A single Type 1 hypervisor","A container orchestration platform (e.g., Kubernetes)","A screened subnet","A WAF"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Orchestration platforms automate scaling, healing, and discovery across many containers - a hypervisor manages VMs, not container fleets; a screened subnet and WAF address network placement and web filtering, not orchestration.$q$),
('q3-047',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A cloud customer worries that a vulnerability in the hypervisor could let another tenant''s workload interfere with theirs. What is this concern called?$q$,$q$["Multi-tenancy risk","VM sprawl","Vendor lock-in","Data sovereignty"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Sharing underlying hardware with other customers creates multi-tenancy risk - a hypervisor flaw could theoretically cross tenant boundaries, which is why providers invest heavily in isolation. Sprawl is about unmanaged proliferation, not shared-tenant risk.$q$),
('q3-048',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$An IoT deployment processes sensor data at regional gateways close to the factory floor instead of sending everything to a central cloud region. What architecture is this and its security trade-off?$q$,$q$["Edge computing; reduces latency but multiplies physically distributed attack surface","Serverless computing; eliminates all attack surface","Client-server; centralizes all risk in one location","Grid computing; has no security implications"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Edge computing pushes processing closer to the data source for latency, at the cost of more physically distributed nodes to secure and monitor - a real trade-off, not a free win.$q$),
('q3-049',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Why can serverless functions complicate post-incident forensics?$q$,$q$["They never execute code","Ephemeral execution environments may terminate before full evidence is captured, unlike a persistent server","They cannot log anything","They run only offline"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Serverless instances spin up and disappear quickly, so volatile runtime evidence can vanish before investigators capture it - logging still works, but the underlying host doesn't persist the way a traditional server does.$q$),
('q3-050',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Moving from on-premises physical servers to public cloud IaaS shifts responsibility for which layer to the provider?$q$,$q$["Application code","Hardware, facilities, and the hypervisor","Data classification","IAM policy configuration"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$In IaaS, the provider takes over hardware, facilities, and the hypervisor; the customer still owns application code, data classification, and IAM configuration above that layer.$q$),
('q3-051',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$A production server's firewall rules differ from what's defined in the team's Terraform repository, because someone made a manual emergency change last week and never updated the code. What is this called, and the fix?$q$,$q$["IaC drift; re-apply the pipeline (and update the source) so live state matches code again","A zero-day; patch immediately","VM sprawl; delete the server","A compensating control; document and accept it"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Manual changes outside the IaC pipeline cause drift between the source-of-truth code and reality; the fix is reconciling them - either updating the code to capture the emergency change or re-applying IaC to revert it.$q$),
('q3-052',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Which virtualization concept means a single kernel exploit on the host could potentially affect every co-located container?$q$,$q$["Hypervisor isolation","Shared kernel in containerization","Full-disk encryption","VLAN tagging"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Containers share the host OS kernel, so a kernel-level compromise threatens every container on that host - unlike VMs, which each run their own OS behind hypervisor-level isolation.$q$),
('q3-053',3,'3.1','matching',2,$q$Match each cloud/infrastructure term to its description.$q$,$q${"left":["Container orchestration","Multi-tenancy","Edge computing","IaC drift"],"right":["Automated scaling/healing of many containers","Multiple customers sharing underlying hardware","Processing data closer to its source","Live infrastructure diverging from source-controlled definitions"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Orchestration automates container fleets; multi-tenancy describes shared cloud hardware; edge computing moves processing near the data source; IaC drift is when reality no longer matches the versioned definition.$q$),
('q3-054',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Which architecture separates network control decisions from the actual packet-forwarding hardware, enabling centralized programmable policy?$q$,$q$["SD-WAN only","Software-Defined Networking (SDN)","A screened subnet","802.1X"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SDN splits the control plane (deciding) from the data plane (forwarding), centralizing policy. SD-WAN is one application of similar ideas to WAN links specifically, not the general concept.$q$),
('q3-055',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Under a Zero Trust model, why is east-west traffic inspection considered as important as perimeter (north-south) inspection?$q$,$q$["East-west traffic is always encrypted already","Attackers who breach the perimeter move laterally between internal hosts, which a perimeter-only model never inspects","East-west traffic never leaves the building","North-south traffic is always safe"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Once inside, attackers move laterally (east-west); a model that only guards the border misses that entire phase of an intrusion. Zero Trust inspects both directions continuously.$q$),
('q3-056',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$An organization exposes ten microservices to partner applications and wants a single place to enforce authentication, rate limiting, and logging instead of duplicating it per service. What should they deploy?$q$,$q$["A NAT gateway","An API gateway","A hub (Layer 1 device)","A patch panel"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An API gateway centralizes cross-cutting API concerns (auth, rate limiting, logging) in one place rather than reimplementing them in every microservice.$q$),
('q3-057',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$Compared to a traditional full-tunnel VPN, what is the key security advantage of ZTNA?$q$,$q$["ZTNA requires no authentication","ZTNA grants broad network-level access like a VPN, just faster","ZTNA grants scoped, per-application access after continuous verification, limiting blast radius versus a VPN's broad network access","ZTNA cannot be used for remote work"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$ZTNA authenticates and authorizes each application session individually and continuously, so a compromised session or credential exposes far less than a VPN's typical broad network-level access.$q$),
('q3-058',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$A data center wants to ensure that even two servers on the same VLAN cannot communicate unless explicitly permitted, rather than relying on subnet-level isolation alone. What technique achieves this?$q$,$q$["VLAN trunking","Microsegmentation","NAT","Port aggregation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Microsegmentation applies policy per-workload, finer-grained than VLAN/subnet boundaries - two hosts in the same VLAN still need explicit permission to talk. Trunking and aggregation address different networking needs.$q$),
('q3-059',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$What is the PRIMARY security benefit of terminating TLS at a reverse proxy in front of a server farm?$q$,$q$["It hides backend server details and centralizes certificate management and WAF inspection","It slows down all traffic intentionally","It removes the need for any encryption","It replaces the need for a firewall entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Centralizing TLS termination at the reverse proxy hides backend topology, simplifies certificate management, and lets a WAF inspect decrypted traffic in one place rather than per server.$q$),
('q3-060',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Which network design principle is violated if a compromised web server can directly query the internal HR database with no intervening controls?$q$,$q$["Defense in depth / segmentation","Data sovereignty","Non-repudiation","Key rotation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Direct, unrestricted reachability between an exposed tier and a sensitive internal system violates segmentation and defense-in-depth principles, letting one compromise cascade freely.$q$),
('q3-061',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$What does a Web Application Firewall inspect that a stateful network firewall (Layer 4) cannot?$q$,$q$["Source and destination IP addresses","Application-layer HTTP/S content such as SQL injection payloads","TCP sequence numbers","Physical cabling faults"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A WAF understands HTTP/S content and can spot application-layer attack patterns (SQLi, XSS) that a Layer 4 firewall, which only sees addresses/ports/state, cannot evaluate.$q$),
('q3-062',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Why might an organization deploy both a network firewall AND a WAF rather than relying on just one?$q$,$q$["Redundancy provides no value here","Each inspects a different layer - network firewall filters by address/port, WAF inspects application content - together giving defense in depth","WAFs replace the need for TLS","Firewalls and WAFs perform identical functions"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$They operate at different layers and catch different attack classes; layering them is defense in depth, not redundant duplication.$q$),
('q3-063',3,'3.2','ordering',2,$q$Order these network controls from BROADEST scope to MOST granular access control.$q$,$q$["Perimeter firewall (whole network boundary)","Network segmentation (VLANs/subnets)","Microsegmentation (per-workload policy)","Host-based firewall (single device)"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Controls narrow in scope from the entire network boundary, to zone-level segments, to per-workload microsegmentation policy, down to a single device's own host firewall - each layer adding finer-grained restriction.$q$),
('q3-064',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$A company must delete a customer's personal data upon request, but that data exists in production, three backups, and an analytics warehouse. What architecture challenge does this illustrate?$q$,$q$["Data sovereignty","The right-to-be-forgotten fulfillment challenge across copies","Data masking","Key escrow"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Honoring an erasure request means finding and removing the data everywhere it was copied - a genuine architectural challenge when backups, replicas, and analytics pipelines all hold copies.$q$),
('q3-065',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$Which DLP enforcement point would catch a user attempting to copy sensitive data to a USB drive?$q$,$q$["Network DLP","Endpoint DLP","Cloud DLP","Email DLP"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Endpoint DLP monitors device-level actions like USB transfers, clipboard use, and printing - network and cloud DLP watch traffic leaving over the wire or into SaaS, not local device actions.$q$),
('q3-066',3,'3.3','mcq',3,$q$In envelope encryption, why is the master key rarely used directly on data?$q$,$q$["It doesn't exist","Limiting the master key's use to encrypting only small data keys reduces its exposure while still scaling to protect unlimited amounts of data","Master keys cannot encrypt anything","It's cheaper to skip envelope encryption entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Restricting the master key to encrypting small data keys (rather than bulk data) minimizes how often and how much it's exposed, while the data key does the heavy lifting - scaling encryption without over-exposing the most critical key.$q$),
('q3-067',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$What does scheduled key rotation primarily limit?$q$,$q$["The cost of encryption","The time window an attacker can exploit if a single key is ever compromised","The number of files that can be encrypted","The need for backups"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Regularly replacing keys shrinks the useful lifetime of any single compromised key, limiting how much data a stolen key can expose before it's retired.$q$),
('q3-068',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$Which data protection method would prevent a database administrator from viewing full customer SSNs in a support tool while still allowing the application to process them?$q$,$q$["Data masking for display, with the real value available only to authorized backend processes","Deleting the SSNs entirely","Disabling the database","Publishing the SSNs publicly"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Masking shows a partial or obscured value to the support tool's viewers while the underlying system retains and processes the full value where legitimately needed.$q$),
('q3-069',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$Why does data classification typically drive DLP policy rather than DLP existing independently?$q$,$q$["DLP rules need to know WHICH data is sensitive enough to block/monitor, and classification supplies that definition","Classification and DLP are unrelated","DLP always blocks everything regardless of sensitivity","Classification only applies to paper documents"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$DLP rules act on content patterns tied to classification levels (e.g., "Restricted" data triggers blocking); without classification defining what's sensitive, DLP has no basis for its policies.$q$),
('q3-070',3,'3.3','mcq',3,$q$A healthcare records system replicates patient data to backups, a reporting warehouse, and a disaster recovery site. From a right-to-be-forgotten standpoint, what must the deletion process account for?$q$,$q$["Only the primary production database","Every location the data was copied to, including backups, replicas, and downstream systems","Only paper records","Nothing - deletion requests don't apply to healthcare data"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A genuine erasure has to track and act on every copy the data reached, not just the system of record - otherwise the "deleted" data still exists in a backup or warehouse.$q$),
('q3-071',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Which plan focuses narrowly on restoring IT systems and data after a disruption, as a supporting piece of a broader continuity effort?$q$,$q$["Business continuity plan","Disaster recovery plan","Risk register","Acceptable use policy"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The DRP is the IT-focused restoration subset; the broader BCP/COOP covers keeping the whole business functioning, of which IT recovery is one part.$q$),
('q3-072',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A continuity test runs the recovery environment alongside live production, comparing outputs without touching real operations. What test type is this?$q$,$q$["Tabletop exercise","Parallel processing test","Full interruption test","Simulation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Parallel processing validates the recovery systems by running them side by side with production - real validation without the risk of actually failing over.$q$),
('q3-073',3,'3.4','mcq',3,$q$Which continuity test provides the HIGHEST confidence but also the highest operational risk, because production is actually stopped?$q$,$q$["Tabletop exercise","Parallel processing test","Full interruption test","Plan review"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$A full interruption test genuinely halts production and fails over for real - the most realistic proof the plan works, but with genuine business disruption risk if it fails.$q$),
('q3-074',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A recovery plan assumes one senior engineer can execute the entire failover procedure alone. What capacity-planning gap does this represent?$q$,$q$["Infrastructure capacity","People capacity - lack of cross-training creates a single point of failure in the recovery process itself","Technology capacity","No gap exists"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A recovery plan dependent on one irreplaceable person is a people-capacity gap - that person being unavailable during an actual incident would break the entire plan.$q$),
('q3-075',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Why must a Recovery Time Objective be set with margin below the Maximum Tolerable Downtime, rather than exactly equal to it?$q$,$q$["They should always be identical","Real recoveries rarely go perfectly; margin absorbs unexpected delays so the business doesn't cross the MTD threshold","MTD is always shorter than RTO by definition","RTO is a legal requirement, MTD is not"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Building margin between RTO and MTD accounts for the reality that recoveries hit unexpected snags - without it, any minor delay breaches the business's actual survival threshold.$q$),
('q3-076',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$What resilience purpose does geographic dispersion of data center sites serve beyond simple redundancy?$q$,$q$["Lower latency only","Ensuring one regional disaster (hurricane, earthquake, grid failure) cannot destroy every redundant site simultaneously","Reducing the number of employees needed","Eliminating the need for backups"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Redundancy alone doesn't help if all copies sit in the same disaster zone; geographic separation ensures a single regional event can't take out every site at once.$q$),
('q3-077',3,'3.4','mcq',3,$q$An organization's DR site can be brought online within 15 minutes using pre-staged core infrastructure, but full capacity takes another two hours to scale up. Which DR strategy does this describe?$q$,$q$["Cold site","Pilot light","Backup and restore only","This is a hot site with zero cost"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A minimal core kept running that then scales up when needed is the pilot light pattern - faster than cold (which starts from nothing) but cheaper than a fully running hot site.$q$),
('q3-078',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Why is testing a continuity plan considered as important as writing it?$q$,$q$["Untested plans routinely fail in ways the paper version never revealed - assumptions, missing steps, and outdated contacts surface only in practice","Testing is a formality with no real value","Plans that are written never need testing","Testing replaces the need for a written plan"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A plan that looks complete on paper often has stale contact info, missing dependencies, or wrong assumptions that only surface when actually exercised - testing is where the plan meets reality.$q$),
('q3-079',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Which resilience technique specifically protects against a single cloud provider's regional or platform-wide outage taking down an entire service?$q$,$q$["RAID 5","Multi-region or multi-cloud deployment","Full-disk encryption","Port security"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Spreading workloads across regions (or providers) is the only listed option that survives a whole-region or whole-platform failure; RAID, encryption, and port security address unrelated risks.$q$),
('q3-080',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A company's BIA determines a payroll system's MTD is 24 hours. Which recovery site tier is the MOST cost-appropriate choice, all else equal?$q$,$q$["Hot site (minutes of RTO, highest cost)","Warm site (hours of RTO, moderate cost)","No recovery plan needed","A cold site with a two-week RTO"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A 24-hour tolerance doesn't justify the expense of a hot site, but a two-week cold-site recovery would breach the MTD entirely - a warm site's hours-scale RTO fits comfortably within the requirement at moderate cost.$q$);
